===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|slang}} A [[hoax]], [[jest]], or [[prank]].
#* {{quote-book|author=Ferdinando Killigrew|title=The Universal Jester: or, A Pocket Companion for the Wits|location=London|publisher=Printed for R. Whitworth, at the Feathers, in the [[w:Poultry, London|Poultry]]; J. Warcus, at the Indian-Queen, opposite the [[w:Mansion House, London|Mansion-House]]; R. Richards, next [[w:Barnard's Inn|Barnard's-Inn]], {{w|Holborn}}; W. Mynors, at the Corner of [[w:Chancery Lane|Chancery-Lane]], Holborn; and W. Heard, at the Philobiblian's-Library, {{w|Piccadilly}}|year=1754|section=title page|oclc=642524111|passage=The universal jester: or, a pocket companion for the wits. Being a choice collection of merry conceits, facetious Drolleries, humorous Waggeries, smart Repartees, pleasant Jokes, Clenchers, Closures, Bon Mots, and '''Humbugs'''; comic Stories, notable Puns, witty Quibbles, and ridiculous Bulls. To which are added, Mr. Puzzlewit's gimcracks ; or, A long String of out-o'th'-way Conundrums, diverting Rebusses, poignant Epigrams, odd and uncommon Epitaphs, &c. &c. All calculated to promote inoffensive Mirth, and divert good Company with Elegance and Taste. Containing more in Number, and greater Variety, than any Book of the Kind yet published. Humbly inscribed to the choice spirits of the age. By Ferdinando Killigrew, Esq.}}
# {{lb|en|slang}} A [[fraud]] or [[sham]]; [[hypocrisy]].
#* {{quote-journal|editor=[J. S. Boone]|title=On Humbug, Pro and Con—and the Art of Puffing|magazine=The Council of Ten|location=London|publisher=Printed for the proprietors, and published by Thomas Wilkie, [[w:Paternoster Row|Paternoster-Row]]; [''et al.'']|volume=I|issue=III|month=August|year=1822|page=327|pageurl=https://books.google.com.sg/books?id=62wEAAAAQAAJ&pg=PA327|oclc=15101686|passage=Look at the affairs of nations on the widest scale—look at their intercourse with each other—look at the manifestoes, by which war is declared—look at the treaties, by which peace is restored—look at the professions of kings, or popes, or generals, or ministers. Is not cant, '''humbug''', hypocrisy, the staple of them all? What is modern diplomacy, but a system of duplicity and deceit?}}
#* {{quote-journal|author=“C.”|title=Humbug|editor=[[w:George Petrie (artist)|George Petrie]]|magazine=The Irish Penny Journal. Containing Original Contributions by Several of the Most Eminent Irish Writers|location=Dublin|published=Printed and published by Gunn and Cameron, at the office of the General Advertiser, No. 6, Church-Lane, College-Green|date=29 August 1840|year_published=1841|volume=I|issue=9|page=67, column 1|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=IefmcuV6F5YC&pg=PA67|oclc=605070893|passage=What is the civility of the landlord and his waiters but '''humbug'''? What the smirking, smiling, ducking and bowing of the shopkeeper, but '''humbug'''? What his sweet and gentle "yes, sirs," and "no, sirs," and "proud to serve you, sirs," but '''humbug'''? You are not goose enough to believe for a moment that he is serious, that he has either the least regard or respect for you.}}
#* {{quote-book|author=J[oseph] H. Bagg|chapter=Magnetism as More Particularly Applied to Man, or What is Commonly Called Animal Magnetism, Clairvoyance, Catalepsy, Palsy, &c.|title=Bagg on Magnetism, or The Doctrine of Equilibrium: Being an Attempt to Prove that not only the Health of Vegetables and Animals, but All Systems and Principles in Nature, Depend on an Equilibrium or Action between Two Extremes, and that the Impulse or Force by which They are Produced, as well as Destroyed, is Owing to the Magnetic Fluids which Operate both at the Same Time, on the Same Object or Principle. [...] In Two Parts|location=Detroit, Mich.|publisher=Bagg and Harmon, printers|year=1845|pages=170–171|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=0uU0AAAAMAAJ&pg=PA171|oclc=565269669|passage=Many times a whole audience will not only be crowded into a small room, but are noisy disbelievers, call it all a '''humbug''', distract the mind of the magnetizer, and added to these, absolutely outwill the magnetizer, in their wish to bring odium upon the science, and carry their points and gain their ends.}}
#* {{quote-book|author=Todd Nathan Thompson|chapter=‘Satire upon All of Us’: The Self-made Man as Confidence Man in P. T. Barnum's America|title=Modest Proposals: American Satire and Political Change from Franklin to Lincoln|series=unpublished Ph.D. in English dissertation|location=Chicago, Ill.|publisher={{w|University of Illinois in Chicago}}|year=2008|page=215|oclc=262556595|passage=[[w:P. T. Barnum|[Phineas Taylor] Barnum]] turned profits detecting '''humbug''', staging '''humbugs''', and in authoring books that present him as a '''humbug'''. In each case he operated by aestheticizing '''humbug''': in writing tongue-in-cheek "reform" literature about avoiding '''humbug''', in creating narratives or mythologies to advertise his own '''humbugs''', and in celebrating in prose his own ability to balance contradictory roles.}}
# {{lb|en|slang}} A [[fraudster]], [[cheat]], or [[hypocrite]].
#* '''1877''' November 24, {{w|Anna Sewell}}, “A Humbug”, in ''[[w:Black Beauty|Black Beauty: His Grooms and Companions. The Autobiography of a Horse]]'', London: [[w:The Jarrold Group|Jarrold and Sons]], 3, Paternoster Buildings, <small>{{w|OCLC}} [http://www.worldcat.org/oclc/228733457 228733457]</small>; republished as ''Black Beauty: The Autobiography of a Horse'', Garden City, N.Y.: [[w:Doubleday (publisher)|Doubleday, Page & Company]], 1922, <small>{{w|OCLC}} [http://www.worldcat.org/oclc/12069677 12069677]</small>, [https://books.google.com/books?id=5UE1AAAAMAAJ&pg=PA151 page 151]:
#*: [I]n a few days my new groom came. He was a tall, good-looking fellow enough; but if ever there was a '''humbug''' in the shape of a groom, Alfred Smirk was the man. He was very civil to me, and never used me ill; in fact, he did a great deal of stroking and patting when his master was there to see it. {{...}} [B]ut as to cleaning my feet, or looking to my shoes, or grooming me thoroughly, he thought no more of that than if I had been a cow.
#* {{quote-book|author=[George] Bernard Shaw|authorlink=George Bernard Shaw|title=[[w:Man and Superman|Man and Superman. A Comedy and a Philosophy]]|location=Westminster, London|publisher=[[w:Constable & Robinson|Archibald Constable & Co., Ltd.]]|year=1903|section=Act III|pages=78–79|pageurl=https://archive.org/stream/mansupermancomed00shawrich#page/79/mode/1up/|oclc=903618586|passage=He means that he has sold out to the parliamentary '''humbugs''' and the bourgeoisie. Compromise! that is his faith.}}
# {{lb|en|slang}} [[nonsense|Nonsense]].
#* '''1992''', {{w|Nina Bawden}}, chapter 3, in ''Humbug'', London: {{w|Victor Gollancz Ltd}}, <small>[[w:International Standard Book Number|ISBN]] [[Special:BookSources/9780575053007|978-0-575-05300-7]]</small>; republished New York, N.Y.: Clarion Books, {{w|Houghton Mifflin Harcourt}}, 1992, <small>[[w:International Standard Book Number|ISBN]] [[Special:BookSources/9780395621493|978-0-395-62149-3]]</small>, page 17:
#*: When they had gone, Ma Potter opened her eyes. She said, "Pay no attention, child. Don't upset yourself. Just '''humbug''', that's all." / "What do you mean?" Cora whispered. {{...}} "You mean, telling lies?" / "Not altogether. Humbuggery is what people talk without thinking. Lies are deliberate. Are you a clever child?"
# {{lb|en|Britain}} A type of [[hard]] [[sweet]] ([[candy]]), usually [[peppermint]] [[flavoured]] with a [[striped]] pattern.
#* {{quote-book|author=Michael Morpurgo|authorlink=Michael Morpurgo|chapter=How to Make Old-fashioned Peppermint Humbugs|title={{w|Private Peaceful}}|location=London|publisher={{w|HarperCollins}}|year=2003|isbn=978-0-00-715007-6|passage='''Humbugs''' are sweet, hard candies with a mild peppermint flavor, which are traditionally made in small batches by hand. {{...}} '''Humbugs''' often feature the old-fashioned peppermint-stripe coloration, dark brown and off-white; are usually oblong or square (about the size of your thumb); {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|author=Jo Cotterill|chapter=White Clover|title=Sweet Hearts|series=Forget Me Not|location=London|publisher=Red Fox|year=2011|page=216|isbn=978-1-84941-217-9|passage=At half past five in the evening, Anpa sat up in bed and said he'd like a packet of '''humbugs'''. Nick and Kate looked at each other and grinned, and Nick immediately picked up his jacket and said he'd go and find some. 'Is he allowed them?' Kate whispered so that Anpa couldn't hear her. / Nick shrugged. 'Can't see how '''humbugs''' will do any harm. But I might get soft mints instead so he doesn't choke on them.'}}
# {{lb|en|US|slang}} Anything [[complicated]], [[offensive]], [[troublesome]], [[unpleasant]] or [[worrying]]; a [[misunderstanding]], especially if [[trivial]].
# {{lb|en|US|African American Vernacular|slang}} A [[fight#Noun|fight]].
# {{lb|en|US|African American Vernacular|slang|dated}} A [[gang]].
# {{lb|en|US|crime|slang}} A [[false]] [[arrest]] on [[trumped-up]] [[charge#Noun|charges]].

